---
import { policyDates } from '../data/legal';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import ContentPage from '../layouts/ContentPage.astro';
---

<ContentPage
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How InsureFacts researches articles from official sources, checks facts, keeps content up to date and stays independent of insurers and advertisers."
  lead="How we research, check and update every article on InsureFacts."
  updated={policyDates.editorialPolicy}
  searchable
>
  <h2>Our principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Facts, not advice.</strong> We explain how insurance works. We never tell readers what they should buy, and we never recommend an insurer or product.</li>
    <li><strong>Official sources first.</strong> Every factual article is based on, and links to, official sources.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain English.</strong> We write clearly and explain technical terms the first time we use them.</li>
    <li><strong>Local accuracy.</strong> Insurance works differently in each country. Each article covers one country and uses its own terms and spelling, such as excess (UK and Australia) or deductible (US and Canada).</li>
    <li><strong>Independence.</strong> No insurer, broker or advertiser can pay for, review or influence what we publish.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where our information comes from</h2>
  <p>We research articles using primary, official sources, including:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>United States:</strong> state insurance departments, the National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC), HealthCare.gov, Medicare.gov and USA.gov.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA), MoneyHelper, the Financial Ombudsman Service and GOV.UK.</li>
    <li><strong>Canada:</strong> provincial and territorial insurance regulators, the Government of Canada (canada.ca and travel.gc.ca), provincial public auto insurers and, for background, the Insurance Bureau of Canada.</li>
    <li><strong>Australia:</strong> ASIC’s Moneysmart, APRA, privatehealth.gov.au, the Australian Financial Complaints Authority (AFCA) and state and territory CTP regulators.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We may also cite legislation and official publications. When we use an industry association as a
    source, we say so. We don’t use insurers’ marketing material, forums or unsourced websites as
    sources.
  </p>

  <h2>How we check facts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every factual statement is checked against the official source cited in the article before it is published.</li>
    <li>We only include figures, such as prices, percentages, limits and dates, when they come from an official source. If something can’t be verified, we leave it out.</li>
    <li>Each article lists its sources in a numbered “Sources” section, with links to the original pages.</li>
    <li>New articles are kept as unpublished drafts until they have been fact-checked.</li>
    <li>We may use software tools, including AI tools, to help with research and drafting. A person checks every article against its cited official sources before it is published.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keeping articles up to date</h2>
  <p>
    Insurance rules, limits and government programs change. Every article shows the date it was last
    updated. We review articles when we learn that the rules have changed, and we aim to review every
    article at least once every {site.reviewAfterMonths} months. If an article hasn’t been updated in
    that time, it shows a notice saying it is being reviewed.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If we get something wrong, we fix it promptly and note significant corrections in our public{' '}
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Advertising</h2>
  <p>
    InsureFacts is funded by advertising served by Google AdSense. Ads are clearly labelled, are kept
    separate from article content, and never appear inside the quick answer or sources sections.
    Advertisers have no influence over our content, and we don’t write sponsored articles.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
